  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA few showers will continue over the Brush Country this afternoon \nand early evening, before tapering off later tonight as PWATs \ndecrease with drier air infiltrating between 850-700mb. However, \nmoisture advection will increase Monday, bringing low to medium (20-\n40%) rain chances along the Coastal Bend.\n\nMoisture continues to surge Tuesday, bringing PWATs near 2.0\", close \nto the 99th percentile for this time of the year. A mid-level \nshortwave developing over the Texas Panhandle will cause PVA to \ndrape over South Texas through midweek. This mid-level lift support \ncombined with above normal moisture will warrant medium to high rain \nchances Tuesday and Wednesday, followed by a shift to the west \ntowards the Rio Grande Plains on Thursday in coordination with the \nmoisture axis and strengthening SW upper-level jet. Instability \nseems to be pretty limited with SBCAPE generally less than 800 J/kg \nbut cap will weaken during the day and allow for a few rumbles of \nthunder mixed in. Total rainfall estimates through Thursday look to \nbe less than an inch, with locally higher up to 2\", most likely \nalong the Coastal Bend. Given rainfall expected earlier in the week, \nthe inland Coastal Plains to Brush Country are now in a marginal \nrisk of flash flooding Thursday, with a slight introduced over the \nnorthern Rio Grande Plains.\n\nA series of additional shortwaves look to follow heading into the \nweekend, which extends our rain chances (at least low rain chances) \nheading into Halloween night. We'll need to keep a close eye on this \nas our safety risk increases with many enjoying the outdoors. High \ntemperatures are to remain in the 80s with lows in the mid to upper \n60s.\n\nLastly, there is increasing concern for minor coastal flooding and \nrip currents along area beaches due to the full moon occurring \ntonight followed by greater swells and persistent east to \nsoutheasterly flow. High tide will likely occur during the afternoon \nhours, with P-ETSS nearing 2.0 ft MSL at Aransas Pass, signaling \npotential minor coastal flooding across various areas of the Middle \nTexas Coast. We will near it this afternoon, wouldn't be surprised \nif an Advisory will be needed with short notice, but more likely \nstarting tomorrow.",
